  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The reaction used for preparation of soap from fat is not alkaline hydrolysis, but rather saponification. In saponification, a triglyceride (fat or oil) is reacted with a strong base, such as sodium hydroxide (or potassium hydroxide, in the presence of heat to produce soap molecules and glycerol as a byproduct. The reaction involves the hydrolysis of the ester bond in the triglyceride by the base to form carboxylate ions (the soap molecules) and glycerol.<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/344eabbd-ae3a-4ab3-bac0-779d90d07511.png\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 259px;\" /></p>",
